## Why This Is Asked

This question tests mastery of rope-like data structures for dynamic sequences with cut-paste operations while maintaining fast range queries.

## Key Concepts

- **Implicit treap / rope**: Uses position as the key rather than explicit values
- **Split and merge operations**: Fundamental primitives for manipulating segments
- **Augmented data**: size and range sum maintained at each node
- **Edge cases**: Properly handling when dest overlaps with [l, r]

## Code Example

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(v) {
    this.v = v;
    this.pr = Math.random();
    this.l = this.r = null;
    this.sz = 1;
    this.sum = v;
  }
}

function upd(t) {
  if (!t) return;
  t.sz = 1 + sz(t.l) + sz(t.r);
  t.sum = t.v + sum(t.l) + sum(t.r);
}

function split(t, k, l, r) {
  if (!t) return [null, null];
  if (sz(t.l) >= k) {
    let [a, b] = split(t.l, k, l, r);
    t.l = b; upd(t);
    return [a, t];
  } else {
    let [a, b] = split(t.r, k - sz(t.l) - 1, l, r);
    t.r = a; upd(t);
    return [t, b];
  }
}

function merge(a, b) {
  if (!a || !b) return a || b;
  if (a.pr < b.pr) {
    a.r = merge(a.r, b); upd(a); return a;
  } else {
    b.l = merge(a, b.l); upd(b); return b;
  }
}

function rangeSum(t, l, r) {
  let [a, bc] = split(t, l);
  let [b, c] = split(bc, r - l + 1);
  let res = sum(b);
  t = merge(a, merge(b, c));
  return res;
}

function moveSegment(t, l, r, dest) {
  let len = r - l + 1;
  let [a, bc] = split(t, l);
  let [b, c] = split(bc, len);
  t = merge(a, c);

  let pos = dest <= l ? dest : dest - len;
  let [x, y] = split(t, pos);\n  return merge(merge(x, b), y);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a scalable data structure for tracking peak occupancy across a large domain with many overlapping intervals—a common problem in campaign management, resource allocation, and scheduling systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Implicit segment tree**: Enables efficient operations over a massive coordinate space (up to 2^31-1) without building a full tree
- **Range add, range max with argmax**: Combines lazy propagation for updates with maintaining both value and position of the maximum
- **Incremental updates**: Insert and erase operations dynamically modify the tree state
- **Handling duplicates**: Multiple intervals covering the same coordinate naturally accumulate via additive weights

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.max = 0;
    this.argMax = 0;
    this.lazy = 0;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }
}

function rangeAdd(node, L, R, ql, qr, delta) {
  if (qr < L || R < ql) return;
  if (ql <= L && R <= qr) {
    node.max += delta;
    node.lazy += delta;
    return;
  }
  push(node);
  const mid = (L + R) >>> 1;
  rangeAdd(node.left, L, mid, ql, qr, delta);
  rangeAdd(node.right, mid + 1, R, ql, qr, delta);
  pull(node);
}
```

